= Causality =

== Overview ==
'''Causality''' concerns relations in which one event, condition, process, or factor is understood as producing, influencing, enabling, or otherwise making a difference to another.

== Definition ==
There is no single uncontested philosophical analysis of causation. Major approaches analyze causal relations through regularities, counterfactual dependence, mechanisms, processes, probabilities, interventions, powers, or combinations of these.

== Scope & importance ==
Causal claims do more than report association. They shape explanation, prediction, responsibility, scientific modeling, intervention, engineering, policy, and everyday reasoning.

== Core concepts ==
Important distinctions include token versus type causation, correlation versus causation, cause versus background condition, direct versus mediated effects, and causal explanation versus prediction.

== Examples ==
A particular drought may be described as contributing to a particular famine; this is a token causal claim. 'Smoking causes cancer' is a general or type-level causal claim whose interpretation involves population evidence and causal structure.

== Applications / implications ==
Causality is central to philosophy of science, statistics, epidemiology, law, machine learning, systems analysis, experimental design, and decision-making.

== Epistemic notes ==
Causation is a contested philosophical concept. BITwiki should represent competing theories explicitly instead of presenting one metaphysical account as settled by definition.

